---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import {
  COMPONENTS,
  MODES,
  GITHUB_REPO,
  GO_MODULE,
  JOSTRACA_VERSION,
} from "../consts";

// The examples below are executed against the pinned package by
// test/examples.test.mjs. A landing page that states a result states the
// generator's, or the test fails.

const EXAMPLE_TREE = `import { Jostraca, Project, Folder, File, Content } from 'jostraca'

await Jostraca().generate({ folder: './out' }, () => {
  Project({ folder: 'acme' }, () => {
    File({ name: 'package.json' }, () => {
      Content('{ "name": "acme" }\\n')
    })
    Folder({ name: 'src' }, () => {
      File({ name: 'index.js' }, () => {
        Content('console.log("acme")\\n')
      })
    })
  })
})`;

const RESULT_TREE = `out/acme/package.json
  { "name": "acme" }
out/acme/src/index.js
  console.log("acme")`;

// EXAMPLE_/RESULT_ so test/examples.test.mjs discovers and RUNS this:
// the landing page's central claim is that a Jostraca template is still a
// valid file of its own type, and a claim on this page is executed like
// every other one. INPUT_ seeds the template into the run's filesystem, so
// the example can show a template that already exists instead of writing
// one first.
const INPUT_TEMPLATE = `tpl/page.html
  <!doctype html>
  <html>
    <head>
      <title>APP_TITLE</title>
    </head>
    <body>
      <h1>APP_NAME</h1>
    </body>
  </html>`;

// The template as the page shows it: INPUT_TEMPLATE with its listing
// indent removed, so the block a reader sees and the file the run reads are
// the same string.
const TEMPLATE_SOURCE = INPUT_TEMPLATE.split("\n").slice(1).map((l) => l.slice(2)).join("\n");

const EXAMPLE_TEMPLATE = `import { Jostraca, Project, File, Fragment } from 'jostraca'

await Jostraca().generate({ folder: './out' }, () => {
  Project({}, () => {
    File({ name: 'index.html' }, () => {
      Fragment({
        from: '../tpl/page.html',
        replace: {
          APP_TITLE: 'acme',
          APP_NAME: 'Acme',
        },
      })
    })
  })
})`;

const RESULT_TEMPLATE = `out/index.html
  <!doctype html>
  <html>
    <head>
      <title>acme</title>
    </head>
    <body>
      <h1>Acme</h1>
    </body>
  </html>`;

const TEMPLATE_DIALECT = `<!doctype html>
<html>
  <head>
    {% block head %}{% endblock %}
  </head>
  <body>
    <h1>{{ app.name }}</h1>
  </body>
</html>`;

const EXAMPLE_MERGE = `import { appendFileSync } from 'node:fs'
import { Jostraca, Project, File, Content } from 'jostraca'

const jostraca = Jostraca({
  existing: { txt: { write: true, merge: true } },
})

const run = (body) => jostraca.generate({ folder: './out' }, () => {
  Project({}, () => File({ name: 'config.sh' }, () => Content(body)))
})

// First run. Then somebody adds a line by hand. Then the generator
// changes a different line and runs again.
await run('PORT=8080\\nHOST=localhost\\n')
appendFileSync('./out/config.sh', 'DEBUG=1\\n')
await run('PORT=9090\\nHOST=localhost\\n')`;

const RESULT_MERGE = `out/config.sh
  PORT=9090
  HOST=localhost
  DEBUG=1`;

---

<Base home>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="ostracon" aria-hidden="true">
      <img
        src="/brand/jostraca-logo.png"
        width="1024"
        height="1536"
        alt=""
      />
    </div>
    <div class="hero-copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">Code generation after the first run</p>
      <h1>Generate it now. Generate it <em>again</em>.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Running a generator once is easy. Run it again after somebody edits the
        output and you have a less cheerful problem. Jostraca calculates every
        file before it writes one, so you can overwrite, preserve, present,
        diff, or merge on the second run.
      </p>
      <p class="lede-2">
        And there is no template syntax to learn. You write ordinary code, or
        you mark up a file that is still valid HTML, Go, or SQL &mdash; so your
        editor, formatter, and linter never notice the difference.
      </p>

      <p class="stacks">
        One generator, <strong>two implementations</strong>: a TypeScript
        package and a Go module, built from one specification and held to the
        same shared test corpus.
      </p>

      <div class="cta">
        <a class="button" href="/docs/tutorial">Start the tutorial</a>
        <a class="button ghost" href="/how-to">How-to guides</a>
        <a class="button ghost" href="/docs">Read the docs</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="taste">
    <div>
      <h2>Declare a tree. Jostraca writes it.</h2>
      <p>
        Components nest like directories. <code>Project</code> contains
        <code>Folder</code>, which contains <code>File</code>, which contains
        <code>Content</code>. They are ordinary function calls, so loops,
        conditionals, and parameters are ordinary JavaScript too. There is no
        template language waiting to become your next programming language.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every name here is a plain string, because a component call is a
        function call and you already have a language for that. Values that
        come from a model, and the <code>$$app.name$$</code> substitution that
        reads them, are in the
        <a href="/docs/tutorial">tutorial</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="panes">
      <figure>
        <figcaption>gen.mjs</figcaption>
        <Code code={EXAMPLE_TREE} lang="js" />
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <figcaption>what it wrote</figcaption>
        <Code code={RESULT_TREE} lang="text" />
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="no-dialect">
    <h2>No template syntax to learn</h2>
    <p class="section-lede">
      Two ways to produce a file. Neither one is a dialect.
    </p>

    <div class="two-ways">
      <div>
        <h3>Write code</h3>
        <p>
          Components are function calls in the language you are already in.
          Iteration is <code>for</code>. Branching is <code>if</code>. Reuse is
          a function. Your debugger, type checker, formatter, and test runner
          keep working, because nothing here is new to them.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Or mark up a real file</h3>
        <p>
          When a file reads better as a file, keep it as one. A marker is just
          a name you chose, sitting where the file already allows text, so the
          template stays valid HTML &mdash; or Go, or SQL, or YAML.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <figure class="template-in">
      <figcaption>tpl/page.html &mdash; an ordinary HTML file</figcaption>
      <Code code={TEMPLATE_SOURCE} lang="html" />
    </figure>

    <div class="panes">
      <figure>
        <figcaption>gen.mjs</figcaption>
        <Code code={EXAMPLE_TEMPLATE} lang="js" />
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <figcaption>what it wrote</figcaption>
        <Code code={RESULT_TEMPLATE} lang="text" />
      </figure>
    </div>

    <p>
      <code>tpl/page.html</code> is valid HTML. A browser renders it, a
      formatter formats it, a linter checks it, and your editor highlights it,
      because every character in it belongs to HTML. <code>APP_TITLE</code> and
      <code>APP_NAME</code> are ordinary text that happens to be replaced. For a
      file where a bare name would not be valid, the
      <a href="/how-to/replace-markers-in-a-template">replace guide</a> covers
      the marker forms that hide inside a comment.
    </p>

    <p>
      Compare the same page written for a template language. This one is
      neither valid HTML nor valid Python: it belongs to a third language that
      only the template engine reads, so highlighting gives up or wants a
      plugin, the formatter cannot parse it, and the only way to find out what
      it produces is to render it.
    </p>

    <figure class="dialect">
      <figcaption>the same page in a template language</figcaption>
      <Code code={TEMPLATE_DIALECT} lang="html" />
    </figure>
    <p>
      <code>$$app.name$$</code> follows the same rule. It sits where the host
      language already expects arbitrary text, reads one value from your model,
      and does nothing else. No conditionals, no loops, no expressions, no
      filters. It cannot grow into a second language, which is the whole reason
      it stays this small.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="second-run">
    <h2>The second run</h2>
    <p>
      A generator that only ever runs on an empty directory is a project
      template. Jostraca is built for the other case: the output is checked in,
      somebody has edited it, and you need to regenerate anyway. {MODES.length}
      modes decide what happens to a file that already exists. Binaries get the first
      three, because a diff of a PNG helps nobody, and the file's extension is
      what picks the set.
    </p>
    <dl>
      {MODES.map((mode) => (
        <div class="mode">
          <dt><code>{mode.name}</code></dt>
          <dd>{mode.blurb}</dd>
        </div>
      ))}
    </dl>

    <div class="panes">
      <figure>
        <figcaption>two runs, with a hand edit in between</figcaption>
        <Code code={EXAMPLE_MERGE} lang="js" />
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <figcaption>what it wrote</figcaption>
        <Code code={RESULT_MERGE} lang="text" />
      </figure>
    </div>

    <p class="more">
      A file carrying the string <code>JOSTRACA_PROTECT</code> is never
      overwritten, whatever the mode says, so a reader can take a generated
      file away from the generator without asking you first. The
      <a href="/docs/reference-options">options reference</a> specifies each
      mode; <a href="/how-to">the how-to guides</a> show them in use.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="components">
    <h2>{COMPONENTS.length} components</h2>
    <p>
      That is the whole vocabulary. Anything else you need is a function you
      write yourself and wrap with <code>cmp()</code>, which makes it a
      component like the rest.
    </p>
    <dl>
      {COMPONENTS.map((component) => (
        <div class="component">
          <dt><code>{component.name}</code></dt>
          <dd>{component.blurb}</dd>
        </div>
      ))}
    </dl>
    <p class="more">
      Every prop and every default is in the
      <a href="/docs/reference-components">component reference</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="parity">
    <h2>Two implementations, one output</h2>
    <p>
      TypeScript is canonical and Go is a maintained port. They are held to
      byte-identical output for the same logical input by a shared,
      language-neutral test corpus that both suites read, so a behaviour
      documented here means the same thing in either language. Where the two
      disagree, TypeScript wins and Go is the one that gets fixed.
    </p>
    <p class="more">
      The Go API, including the places where Go idiom requires a different structure, is in
      the <a href="/docs/reference-go">Go reference</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="install">
    <h2>Install</h2>
    <div class="panes">
      <figure>
        <figcaption>npm</figcaption>
        <Code code={`npm install jostraca@${JOSTRACA_VERSION}`} lang="sh" />
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <figcaption>Go</figcaption>
        <Code code={`go get ${GO_MODULE}`} lang="sh" />
      </figure>
    </div>
    <p class="more">
      MIT licensed. Source, issues and the design record are on
      <a href={GITHUB_REPO} rel="noopener">GitHub</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  section {
    padding: 56px 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .hero {
    text-align: center;
    padding: clamp(64px, 10vw, 112px) 0;
  }
  .hero-copy {
    margin: 0 auto;
  }
  /* Centre every block in the hero, structurally.
     global.css caps every <p> at var(--measure), so a paragraph here is
     NARROWER than the hero and, without auto side margins, sits flush left
     while its text centres inside it -- which reads as a misalignment
     rather than as a bug. .lede and h1 each carried their own
     margin-left/right: auto and looked fine; .eyebrow and .lede-2 were
     added later without them and did not. Setting it once on the children
     means the next paragraph added here is centred by default instead of
     by remembering. */
  .hero-copy > * {
    margin-left: auto;
    margin-right: auto;
  }
  section:last-of-type {
    border-bottom: none;
  }

  .hero h1 {
    max-width: 18ch;
    margin-left: auto;
    margin-right: auto;
    font-size: clamp(3rem, 7.5vw, 5.6rem);
    line-height: 0.96;
  }
  .hero h1 em {
    color: var(--secondary);
    font-weight: inherit;
  }
  .eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 18px;
    color: var(--secondary);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  .lede {
    margin-top: 26px;
    font-size: 1.12rem;
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin-left: auto;
    margin-right: auto;
  }

  .ostracon {
    margin: 0 auto 28px;
  }
  .ostracon img {
    display: block;
    width: 128px;
    height: auto;
    margin: 0 auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
    box-shadow: 0 8px 24px var(--shadow);
  }
  .cta {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 12px;
    margin-top: 28px;
    justify-content: center;
  }

  .taste,
  .second-run,
  .components,
  .parity,
  .install {
    display: block;
  }

  .taste > div:first-child p,
  .second-run > p,
  .components > p,
  .parity > p {
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  /*
    Source above, result below. Two columns looked tidier and clipped the
    first line of every example behind a scrollbar at 1280px, which is a
    poor first thing for a landing page to do with its code. The short
    install pair keeps its columns below.
  */
  /* The two-ways split above the side-by-side templates. Same
     one-column-then-two behaviour as .panes, so the section reads in a
     single column on a phone. */
  .lede-2 {
    margin-top: 12px;
    opacity: 0.85;
  }

  @media (min-width: 860px) {
    .no-dialect .panes {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    }
  }

  .dialect {
    margin: 20px 0 0;
  }

  .no-dialect .section-lede {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    opacity: 0.85;
    margin-top: -4px;
  }

  .two-ways {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: 20px;
    margin-top: 20px;
  }

  .two-ways h3 {
    margin: 0 0 6px;
    font-size: 1rem;
  }

  .two-ways p {
    margin: 0;
  }

  @media (min-width: 700px) {
    .two-ways {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
      gap: 28px;
    }
  }

  .panes {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: 16px;
    margin-top: 24px;
  }
  .install .panes {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 20px;
  }
  .panes figure {
    margin: 0;
    min-width: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
  }
  .panes figcaption {
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    color: var(--ink-faint);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    margin-bottom: 6px;
  }
  .panes pre {
    margin: 0;
  }

  dl {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(260px, 1fr));
    gap: 4px 28px;
    margin: 24px 0 0;
  }
  .mode,
  .component {
    display: flex;
    gap: 10px;
    align-items: baseline;
    padding: 6px 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  dt {
    margin: 0;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
  }
  dd {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    font-size: 0.92rem;
  }

  .more {
    margin-top: 20px;
    max-width: var(--measure);
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }

  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .hero {
      padding: 56px 0 64px;
    }
    .hero h1 {
      font-size: clamp(2.8rem, 14vw, 4.4rem);
    }
    .install .panes {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
